  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SERVIR Central America Hub (Phase 3)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Central America Regional):  Central America&apos;s population, ecosystems, infrastructure, agricultural production, and industries are highly vulnerable to extreme weather events. The region&apos;s terrestrial and aquatic biodiversity is further threatened by rising deforestation, land conversion, wildfires, species overexploitation, and pollution-all of which are intensified by the impacts of climate change. By supporting access to geospatial data and improving capacity to apply it, this activity is uniquely suited to support Central American institutions, communities, and citizens to make informed decisions that mitigate their vulnerability to environmental degradation and climate change.Launched in July 2024, the SERVIR-CA Hub will utilize satellite data and geospatial technologies to co-develop demand-driven tools and services while providing targeted training and technical assistance to enhance decision-making at local, national, and regional levels. Through SERVIR-CA, USAID will collaborate with governments, academic institutions, the private sector, local communities, and other key partners to address critical issues related to agriculture and food security, air quality and public health, ecosystem and carbon management, water security, and weather and climate resilience. This initiative will contribute to regional security and prosperity while helping to reduce the drivers of irregular migration.SERVIR is a joint initiative of NASA and USAID, combining NASA&apos;s extensive library of Earth observation data with USAID&apos;s on-the-ground partnerships and development expertise. Through this activity&apos;s efforts to build capacity to integrate data into local and national decision-making, USAID will support RDCS RDO 3 and RCS Pillar I.</narrative>
  </description>
